# .symbol-citation-baseline — the doc SYMBOL law's three committed lists (#369).
#
# The law lives in `packages/server/src/doc-citation-law.test.ts`; read its
# "doc symbol law" docblock for the grammar, the three arms and the rejected
# alternatives. This file carries only the data, and it is a separate file for
# a mechanical reason, not a stylistic one: arm 1 decides "does this symbol
# still exist" by extracting identifiers from the NON-COMMENT text of this
# repo's sources, string literals included (an env var such as
# `RHIZOMORPH_DATA_DIR` appears nowhere else). A TypeScript const holding these
# names would therefore put every one of them INTO arm 1's token set, and each
# would resolve as 'present' against its own baseline entry — the list would
# silently certify itself and both honesty checks below would pass vacuously.
# Shape copied from `.citation-prior-tracker`, which is the same idea one law
# over.
#
# Sections are `[name]` on a line of their own. `#` comments and blank lines
# are ignored. Fields are whitespace-separated; only the last field may
# contain spaces.
#
# [renamed-away] — EXPECTED VIOLATIONS. A symbol cited by `<file>` that this
#   repo once declared and no longer has. Every entry is re-checked on every
#   run: an entry whose file no longer cites it, or which now resolves, is
#   itself a failure, so the list cannot drift. Removing entries is the
#   destination — this law is the instrument, and correcting the documents is
#   follow-up work.
#   Keyed on the PAIR, not the symbol: a symbol cited from three files is three
#   entries, because each citation is a separate wrong claim and each is fixed
#   separately. 40 entries over 34 distinct symbols.
#     <citing-file>  <symbol>  <the commit that removed it, and what it did>
#
# [historical] — NOT VIOLATIONS, and NOT debt. A symbol `<file>` names ON
#   PURPOSE precisely because this repo removed it: a sentence recording a
#   deletion, a comment warning about a spelling that used to exist. The row
#   is a RULING, not a chore — nothing here is expected to be removed, which
#   is exactly what separates it from [renamed-away] above, whose entries are
#   debt against documents that have something to correct. Mechanically the
#   two are checked the same way: an entry whose file stops citing it, or
#   whose symbol comes back, reddens.
#   Opened by the review of #369. `docs/roadmap.md`'s `NOT_YET_SWEPT` line is
#   the case that opened it, and it is the one the path law next door had
#   already foreseen — its docblock declines to widen to `.mjs` for exactly
#   this reason, "the law needs a way to say 'cited from history, on purpose'
#   … that mechanism does not exist yet". Filing such a line under
#   [renamed-away] would send the next reader to "fix" a true sentence.
#     <citing-file>  <symbol>  <why the citation names it on purpose>
#
# [not-ours] — NOT VIOLATIONS, pinned so the category cannot widen in silence.
#   A cited token that is identifier-shaped but was never declared in
#   `packages/` history: an external API, an env var this repo does not read, a
#   key in someone else's config or JSON, or a spelling this repo's own prose
#   warns about without ever having had. Arm 3 would skip these silently; the
#   issue's definition of done says "not silently skipped", so they are listed.
#   A member that becomes ours, or becomes renamed-away, reddens.
#     <symbol>  <a file that cites it>
#
# measured: 2026-09-13 tree=aaf9a50c candidates=2187 renamed-away=40 historical=1 not-ours=71
#   Re-derived at the review of #369, on the tree this actually lands on. The
#   previous stamp read `a3c5b305`, a tree two rebases old — a stale provenance
#   claim inside the artefact built to catch stale claims.

